\section*{Large Goemans–Linial gaps for uniform sparsest cut}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 117; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that some growing uniform sparsest-cut instances have Goemans--Linial gaps at least $c\sqrt{\log n}/(\log\log n)^3$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 117; 2026-09-24), shared Hilbert features and rounded charts approximate a continuous parameter metric. Triangle defects are controlled both globally and within charts, and multiplicities replace the parameter law by exactly unit pair demands without losing the gap scale.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 117; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 117; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: some growing uniform sparsest-cut instances have Goemans--Linial gaps at least $c\sqrt{\log n}/(\log\log n)^3$.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{UniformSparsestCut.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.UniformSparsestCut.mainGap}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

